  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A monosaccharide is a polyhydroxy aldehyde or ketone, with hydrogen and oxygen in the ratio 2 : 1: CₙH₂ₙOₙ.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Glucose is a six-carbon sugar: C₆H₁₂O₆.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sucrose is two sugar units joined together, so it is a disaccharide.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sucrose is glucose joined to fructose, so hydrolysis gives one of each.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Maltose is a dimer of glucose, so hydrolysis gives two glucose molecules.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lactose, the sugar of milk, is glucose joined to galactose.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Both anomeric carbons of sucrose are locked in the glycosidic bond, so it cannot open to an aldehyde.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The aldehyde group of a reducing sugar reduces Cu²⁺ to Cu₂O, which is the brick-red precipitate.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The blue copper(II) solution is reduced to a brick-red precipitate of copper(I) oxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Iodine gives a blue-black colour with the amylose coil of starch.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Glycogen is the animal storage polysaccharide, more branched than starch.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Human digestive enzymes are specific for α-glucose links; the β links of cellulose pass through as fibre.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Both are built from glucose units; only the linkage differs.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    (C₆H₁₀O₅)ₙ + nH₂O → nC₆H₁₂O₆: the product is glucose.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Every amino acid has –NH₂ and –COOH on the same carbon, with a side chain R.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The –CO–NH– link formed with the loss of water is the peptide (amide) bond.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A molecule of water is eliminated and the two amino acids join — condensation.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Biuret reagent, made from sodium hydroxide and copper(II) sulphate, gives a violet colour with a protein.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The blue copper(II) solution turns violet in the presence of peptide bonds.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Shaking the sample with water gives a milky emulsion, or rubbing it on paper gives a translucent stain.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The white anhydrous salt takes up water of crystallisation and becomes the blue pentahydrate.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Heat, acid, alcohol and heavy-metal ions break the weak forces holding the fold, but the sequence survives.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cooling slows a reaction but does not unfold a protein; the other three break the weak bonds that hold the shape.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An enzyme is a biological catalyst, and almost all enzymes are proteins.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The shape of the active site admits only its own substrate, which is the lock-and-key idea.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Body temperature, about 37 °C, is the optimum; above about 45 °C the enzyme is denatured.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Amylase hydrolyses the α-1,4 links of starch, and maltase then splits maltose into glucose.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zymase catalyses the fermentation of glucose to ethanol and carbon dioxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    In air the ethanol is oxidised further, and the yield of alcohol falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Glucose gives ethanol and carbon dioxide; the gas is what raises bread.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    DCPIP is blue and is reduced to a colourless compound by ascorbic acid.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Proteins contain about 16 % nitrogen by mass, which is why the conversion factor for food analysis is 6.25.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    m(protein) = 0.32/0.16 = 2.0 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Fructose is a single sugar unit; the others are di- or polysaccharides.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ring opens to the aldehyde, and that aldehyde is what reduces Benedict's reagent.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Glycogen has more frequent branch points than amylopectin, which allows faster release of glucose.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cellulose gives strength to plant cell walls; starch and glycogen are storage polysaccharides.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Boiling with acid or alkali, or the action of a protease, breaks the peptide bonds and sets the amino acids free.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A fat is a triglyceride: glycerol esterified with three fatty acids.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sucrose is a disaccharide of glucose and fructose, formed from two C₆H₁₂O₆ units with the loss of one water: C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁.
  ]

]

